Benjamini-Hochberg correction across all $m{=}30$ boundary tests (10 C4 configurations $\times$ 3 boundary pairs) yields $q_{\max} = 0.003 \ll 0.05$.
Under an extreme upper bound of $m{=}185$ (all pairwise depth comparisons across all configurations), $q_{\max} = 0.019 < 0.05$.
Multiple testing therefore does not affect any $\Kempirical$ determination.

\subsection{Qualitative Examples}
\label{sec:qualitative}

The following three cases illustrate depth-dependent text degradation under continuation-mode prompting with Qwen-2.5-1.5B on C4 ($\Kempirical{=}2$) and arXiv ($\Kempirical{=}3$) seeds.
Documents were selected to illustrate representative degradation patterns: highest feature contrast between depths~0 and~1 (Case~A), factual drift (Case~B), and domain-specific template collapse (Case~C).
Individual features may exhibit non-monotonic variation at the single-document level; the classifier operates on the joint 15-dimensional feature vector, where aggregate patterns remain discriminative.

